This repository's changes to upstream's cfg/k3s-cis-1.9 at the pinned commit (ADR 0025, amendment
2026-10-10), applied by app/kube-bench/Dockerfile with `git apply`: a bump whose upstream text moved
fails the build here instead of running a stale override. Everything above the first `diff` line is
commentary, which `git apply` ignores.

  1.2.26                  type: skip, with the reason as the remediation, in upstream's own form for
                          its k3s skips ("Not Applicable." and why). kube-bench reports it INFO with
                          type "skip"; the posture API lists a skipped check whose remediation opens
                          with "Not Applicable." as not applicable, with the rest as the reason
                          (app/api/internal/posture). `make validate` (scripts/check-cis-na.sh)
                          fails if the k3s role ever configures etcd while this skip stands.
  1.1.9, 1.1.10           the CNI configuration directory this node really uses, /etc/cni/net.d
                          (Cilium's 05-cilium.conflist; containerd's default, which k3s keeps when
                          flannel is off), not the host-local IPAM directory /var/lib/cni/networks,
                          which Cilium's own IPAM does not create. The CronJob mounts it read-only.
                          An empty or missing directory still FAILs.
  1.1.11                  the datastore directory k3s really uses (server/db, kine's SQLite here),
                          not the etcd default path that does not exist on k3s.
  3.1.3                   automated: PASS only if the API server runs with
                          --enable-bootstrap-token-auth=false (ansible/roles/k3s).
  3.2.2                   automated: JSONPath tests over the node's audit policy
                          (ansible/roles/k3s/templates/audit-policy.yaml.j2). They check: the first
                          rule is an unconditional Metadata rule naming Secrets and
                          authentication.k8s.io; there are exactly three None rules, at positions
                          1-3, each pinned (the control-plane users' and the nodes' reads; events,
                          endpoints, endpointslices, leases); no Request or RequestResponse rule
                          names Secrets or ConfigMaps; pods/exec and pods/portforward are named by a
                          RequestResponse rule, pods/proxy and services/proxy by a Metadata rule; the
                          last rule is an unconditional Metadata catch-all. They do not evaluate
                          first-match order between the other rules: tests/golden/audit-policy.sh does.
  4.2.12                  the closing quote of k3s's log line is dropped before the flags are read:
                          k3s logs the kubelet's flags sorted inside msg="...", --tls-cipher-suites
                          comes last, and upstream's audit read the quote as part of the last suite
                          name, so the check could never pass.
  4.2.13                  --pod-max-pids must be at least 1 (upstream accepts any value, -1 = none).

Regenerate: check out upstream at KUBE_BENCH_COMMIT, edit cfg/k3s-cis-1.9, `git diff` below this
header.

